      "Interpretation of both FA and ICGA depends on the pattern of a finding's evolution across the full transit sequence — leakage, staining, pooling, window defect, and blocking are each defined by their behavior over time, not by appearance on a single frame — and on correctly localizing the abnormality to the retinal versus choroidal circulation. Fluorescein's small size and rapid leakage through compromised vessels give FA outstanding sensitivity for retinal vascular disease (leakage, nonperfusion, neovascularization) but relatively limited ability to resolve the choroidal circulation clearly, since the normally fenestrated choriocapillaris leaks fluorescein diffusely and obscures fine choroidal detail. ICG's high protein binding and the superior tissue penetration of near-infrared light give ICGA the opposite strength: excellent visualization of choroidal vessels, polypoidal lesions, and pathology hidden beneath blood, pigment, or serous fluid that is essentially invisible on FA — most concretely, polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy cannot be reliably diagnosed without ICGA. OCT and OCT angiography have supplanted dye-based angiography for many routine, repeated monitoring tasks (e.g., following known, quiescent choroidal neovascularization) because they are faster and non-invasive, but FA and ICGA remain necessary whenever true leakage activity, wide-field perfusion status, inflammatory vascular wall involvement, or choroidal vascular pathology such as polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy needs to be established, since neither structural OCT nor motion-contrast OCT angiography can substitute for that specific functional and dye-transit information.",
